<a id="deadlock"></a>
## Two workflows

Example input:

```yaml
# file: .github/workflows/ci.yml
# select: concurrency-deadlock
on: push

concurrency:
  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.sha }}

jobs:
  build:
    uses: ./.github/workflows/build.yml
```

Example file: .github/workflows/build.yml

```yaml
on: workflow_call

concurrency:
  group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.sha }}

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - run: make
```

Output:

```
.github/workflows/ci.yml:6:10: the concurrency group "${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.sha }}" is the same as the group of the workflow of .github/workflows/build.yml (line 4), which job "build" calls. the called workflow runs inside this one with its `github` context, so both groups are one: this run holds the group while it waits for the called workflow, which waits for the group. GitHub detects the deadlock and cancels the run. give one of the groups a part of its own, for example a suffix such as "-caller" [concurrency-deadlock]
  |
6 |   group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.sha }}
  |          ^~~
```

